From 5c5a487b764c6358ca3710e84ee70a7157ef6cf9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 21:14:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 14/23] egl: do not report a failed wglMakeCurrent() as success

WglDisplay::makeCurrent() retries wglMakeCurrent() while it fails with
no error code (a hibernation workaround), but its loop also stops on the
first failure that does set an error, and then returns true because the
retry counter has not run out. A real failure, for example ERROR_BUSY
(0xaa) when the context is current on another thread, is therefore
reported as success. eglMakeCurrent() records the context as current for
the thread while Windows left no context current, so every later GL call
on that thread silently does nothing: glCreateShader() returns 0 and
glGetShaderiv() / glGetShaderInfoLog() leave their outputs untouched.

Only retry the error-less failure and return false for any other one.
A failed release still counts as done, as wglMakeCurrent() leaves no
context current after a failure.

Returning the failure alone would leave a stale state behind:
eglMakeCurrent() returns EGL_BAD_ACCESS but keeps the previous context
recorded as current, although wglMakeCurrent() made it not current. A
caller that restores the previous context afterwards, such as
DisplaySurfaceGlContextHelper::teardownContext(), then finds nothing to
restore, and the thread's later GL calls still go nowhere. So when the
native bind fails, release the previous context explicitly and record
that the thread has no current context. EGL and the native state then
agree, and the next eglMakeCurrent() of the previous context binds it
again.

The release is done for every back end, not only WGL. GLX and CGL keep
the previous context current when a bind fails, which is also what the
EGL specification describes for a failed eglMakeCurrent(), so there the
release is a change rather than a confirmation. The outcome is the same
on every back end, though: the thread has no current context, EGL
records exactly that, and a caller that restores the previous context,
like teardownContext(), binds it again. eglMakeCurrent() cannot know
whether the native failure kept the old context, and keeping it on
Windows would take another native bind that can fail the same way, so
one well-defined state after a failure is simpler and safer.

EglContext::~EglContext() asserted that binding the context for its
cleanup succeeds. That bind can now report a real failure, so log it
instead of asserting.

Test: a debugger makes wglMakeCurrent() fail with a context that is
      current on another thread (the ERROR_BUSY case) while the calling
      thread has its own context current: eglMakeCurrent() fails, the
      release follows, and teardownContext() binds the thread's
      previous context again (wglMakeCurrent() returns 1); the thread's
      later binds work. Tested on Windows only.

